## Why This Is Asked

Assesses the ability to architect privacy-aware computer vision systems under strict edge constraints while balancing accuracy and privacy guarantees. Requires concrete architecture, differentiable privacy terms, and rigorous evaluation plans.

## Key Concepts

- Privacy-preserving CV and de-identification modules
- Differentiable privacy loss and threat-model-aware evaluation
- Edge/on-device inference and latency budgeting
- Trade-offs between detection accuracy and privacy guarantees

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight total loss combining detection accuracy and privacy
function computeTotalLoss(detectionPred, privacyPred, targets) {
    const detectionLoss = computeDetectionLoss(detectionPred, targets);
    const privacyLoss = computePrivacyLoss(privacyPred, targets.sensitiveRegions);

    // Weighted combination with privacy budget constraint
    return detectionLoss + lambdaPrivacy * privacyLoss;
}

// Differentiable de-identification module
class PrivacyModule {
    obfuscate(features, sensitiveRegions) {
        // Apply learnable obfuscation to sensitive regions
        return features.map((feat, i) => 
            sensitiveRegions[i] ? this.anonymize(feat) : feat
        );
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design cross-modal, real-time computer vision pipelines that integrate on-device adaptation with differentiable geometry for robotic manipulation and planning in unstructured environments.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time monocular depth and surface normals estimation from a single RGB camera
- Differentiable probabilistic mesh prediction integrated with motion planning
- Few-shot (5-shot) on-device adaptation using synthetic pre-training followed by real-world fine-tuning
- Multi-task learning with shared encoders and task-specific heads

## Code Example

```javascript
// Core inference pipeline sketch
function forwardPass(frame) {
  const features = sharedEncoder(frame);
  const depth = depthHead(features);
  const normals = normalHead(features);
  const mesh = meshHead(features);
  return { depth, normals, mesh };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you quantify uncertainty in the mesh predictions to ensure safe manipulation?
- What strategies would you use to handle reflective or texture-less surfaces where depth estimation typically fails?
- How does the system handle dynamic scene changes between adaptation and deployment?

## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design a real-time monocular system integrating segmentation, 3D pose estimation with scene priors, grasp-point planning, and physics-based stability reasoning—plus efficient edge adaptation with minimal shots.

## Key Concepts

- Monocular 3D pose estimation using environmental priors
- Grasp-point prediction in cluttered scenes
- Differentiable physics modules for stability evaluation
- On-device few-shot adaptation strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode outline for the model heads
function forward(img, shelfPrior) {
  const features = backbone(img);
  const segmentation = segmentationHead(features);
  const pose = poseHead(features, shelfPrior);
  const grasp = graspHead(features, pose);
  const stability = physicsPrior(grasp, pose);
  return { segmentation, pose, grasp, stability };
}
```

